= Solution
{parent=i}

Let $P$ be the $3$-by-$3$ evaluation <matrix> $P_{i,\ell+1}=p_\ell(\lambda_i)$. The kernel evaluation <matrix> is the <Gram matrix> $PP^T$, so <multiplicativity of the determinant> gives
$$
\det[K_3(\lambda_i,\lambda_j)]_{i,j=1}^3
=\det(PP^T)=(\det P)^2.
$$
Because the <polynomials> are <monic> of consecutive degrees, their coefficient <matrix> relative to $1,x,x^2$ is a <triangular matrix> with unit diagonal. It has <determinant> one, so the <Vandermonde determinant invariance under monic basis change> gives
$$
\det P=\det\begin{pmatrix}
1&\lambda_1&\lambda_1^2\\
1&\lambda_2&\lambda_2^2\\
1&\lambda_3&\lambda_3^2
\end{pmatrix}
=\prod_{i<j}(\lambda_j-\lambda_i).
$$
Squaring removes the ordering sign. Thus the <Gaussian unitary ensemble> <joint probability density function> has precisely the kernel form
$$
\boxed{\rho_3(\boldsymbol\lambda)
=c_3\det[K_3(\lambda_i,\lambda_j)]_{i,j=1}^3
\exp\!\left(-\frac12\sum_i\lambda_i^2\right).}
$$
No <orthogonality> of the <polynomials> is needed for this step.

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Use the <inner product> $\langle f,g\rangle=\mathbb E[f(Z)g(Z)]$ for a <standard normal random variable> $Z$. The degree-zero <monic polynomial> is $p_0=1$. Write $p_1=x+a$ and $p_2=x^2+bx+c$. The conditions
$$
0=\langle p_1,1\rangle=a,\qquad
0=\langle p_2,1\rangle=1+c,\qquad
0=\langle p_2,x\rangle=b
$$
use $\mathbb EZ=\mathbb EZ^3=0$ and $\mathbb EZ^2=1$. Hence these first three <probabilists' Hermite polynomials> are
$$
\boxed{p_0(x)=1,\qquad p_1(x)=x,\qquad p_2(x)=x^2-1.}
$$
The <Gaussian fourth moment> is $\mathbb EZ^4=3$, so their squared <norms> are
$$
\boxed{h_0=1,\qquad h_1=1,\qquad
h_2=\mathbb E(Z^2-1)^2=3-2+1=2,}
\qquad
h_\ell=\int_{\mathbb R}p_\ell(x)^2\frac{e^{-x^2/2}}{\sqrt{2\pi}}\,dx.
$$

= b
{parent=2}
